Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
let's go  over the last two years fitness  sensation peloton has been on a wild  ride  even before the pandemic the  subscriber-based company had amassed a  cult following  and at the height of the pandemic shares  reached all-time highs no one is getting  left behind pelotonis for anyone who  wants it but then almost suddenly the  world and peloton's luck shifted gyms  and offices began reopening as lockdown  restrictions were lifted and over a  single year the company lost nearly all  of its pandemic stock market gains  here's how peloton the once high-flying  fitness craze spun out losing billions  of dollars in market share in the  process  peloton launched a decade ago as a  stationary bike company that had big  aspirations for democratizing the  fitness world  peloton had this vision pre-pandemic  even that everyone who had broadband  internet would want a peloton and could  afford a peloton laura foreman covers  technology for the herd on the street  column at the wall street journal  she says that the company's subscribers  were primarily wealthy consumers with  the means to invest in high-end exercise  equipment with a monthly subscription  fee this is a company that wanted to be  accessible but gained a cult following  because it was the opposite of that it  was aspirational and it really became  this whole lifestyle that's one reason  and then the second reason is just  people were obsessed with the content  but the company knew that to continue to  grow it needed to expand its user base  and in early 2020 as the world shut down  demand for home exercise equipment  surged people weren't traveling they  weren't going outside so maybe they had  some of that budget that they could sort  of allocate to fitness pelton come on  let's get this workout started and with  the surge in demand the fitness company  became a big success its market value  grew considerably reaching almost 50  billion dollars in early 2021 up from  just seven billion dollars two years  prior over a single year the company  gained almost a million new subscribers  but even in the midst of its success  during the pandemic peloton was already  facing issues by late 2020 as demand for  its products soared the company was  racing to fill orders but it was  struggling to keep up complaints from  customers about delays and missed  deliveries mounted and peloton's share  price began to plunge they invested a  ton in sustaining this monster growth  that was never going to be sustainable  and then in march 2021 a fatal incident  occurred involving peloton's high-end  treadmill tragic peloton accident that  resulted in the death of a child the  company is issuing new warnings to  owners in the wake of the accident the  incident pushed peloton to recall its  treadmills and this move was costly  peloton estimated that recalling and  halting sales of its treadmills would  cost the company around 165 million  dollars over a single quarter in 2021 it  really through cold water on the notion  that peloton could continue to grow  maybe they were growing too quickly or  they weren't really putting as much  thought into every product trouble for  the company continued into the summer of  2021. as vaccines became more widely  available and gyms began reopening the  company faced mounting challenges when  the world opened up and all the people  who could afford a peloton already had  one you saw demand start to decline in  the attempt to appeal to a broader  audience peloton slashed prices but as  demand continued to fall the move ended  up hurting the company's bottom line by  the end of the year peloton's stock had  plummeted losing more than  three-quarters of its value from its  december 2020 high which erased most of  its pandemic era gains the company's  market value had dropped about 85  percent then in early 2022 the company  changed its price decision and added  hundreds of dollars in fees to its bike  and treadmill a move that came as the  company grappled with waning demand  and for some investors the situation  looked dire around the same time news  broke that peloton was temporarily  halting production of its connected  fitness products amid a company-wide  hiring freeze peloton the shares  reopened after being halted twice  they're now down more than 20 percent  just weeks later under pressure from an  activist investor the company announced  it would be replacing its ceo and  cutting 2 800 jobs about 20 percent of  its workforce in an effort to get back  on track the changes announced are  exactly the types of things that  investors and analysts want to be seeing  it's you know a new a new manager um a  new uh cost structure foreman says it's  no coincidence that the new ceo barry  mccarthy was the cfo of netflix and  spotify both lower price  subscription-based companies some think  barry's gonna come in and  completely change the company from a  company that was aspirational to a  company that truly is accessible with  really  lower price subscription  models  others think there's really no hope for  the company and it just needs to get  sold but one thing is for certain the  road ahead for peloton is a steep climb  [Music]  you

